This version builds runs as numbers arrive. For a new value, the map stores the length of the run touching its left side and the run touching its right side. The new value joins both sides, and only the two outside boundaries need their lengths updated.

For instance, adding `4` between `[1, 2, 3]` and `[5, 6]` combines lengths 3 and 2 into a run of length 6. Duplicate values are ignored.

```java
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

class Solution {
    public int longestConsecutive(int[] nums) {
        Map<Integer, Integer> boundaryLengths = new HashMap<>();
        int longest = 0;

        for (int number : nums) {
            if (boundaryLengths.containsKey(number)) {
                continue;
            }

            int leftLength = boundaryLengths.getOrDefault(number - 1, 0);
            int rightLength = boundaryLengths.getOrDefault(number + 1, 0);
            int totalLength = leftLength + 1 + rightLength;

            boundaryLengths.put(number, totalLength);
            boundaryLengths.put(number - leftLength, totalLength);
            boundaryLengths.put(number + rightLength, totalLength);

            longest = Math.max(longest, totalLength);
        }

        return longest;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: O(n) average, since each map operation is average constant time.
Space complexity: O(n). The input array is not changed.

This keeps the first version straightforward. For each distinct number, the code checks whether it is followed by `number + 1`, then `number + 2`, and so on. A set makes those checks easy, but this code does not skip numbers that already have a predecessor, so it can repeat work on long runs.

That makes it useful for understanding the problem, though it is slower than the start-of-run method. The empty input is handled before the set is scanned.

```csharp
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution
{
    public int LongestConsecutive(int[] nums)
    {
        if (nums.Length == 0)
        {
            return 0;
        }

        HashSet<int> numbers = new HashSet<int>(nums);
        int longest = 1;

        foreach (int number in numbers)
        {
            int length = 1;
            int next = number + 1;

            while (numbers.Contains(next))
            {
                length++;
                next++;
            }

            if (length > longest)
            {
                longest = length;
            }
        }

        return longest;
    }
}
```

For an input such as `[1, 1, 2]`, the set leaves `{1, 2}`, so duplicates are counted only once.

Time complexity: O(n squared) in the worst case because many values can scan the same run.
Space complexity: O(n) for the hash set.

Sorting makes the scattered values easier to scan. After sorting, equal values are ignored, a value exactly one larger continues the current run, and any larger gap starts a new run.

For example, `[100, 4, 200, 1, 3, 2]` becomes `[1, 2, 3, 4, 100, 200]`, so the best length is 4. This changes the input array because the sort is done in place.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int longestConsecutive(vector<int>& nums) {
        if (nums.empty()) {
            return 0;
        }

        sort(nums.begin(), nums.end());
        int currentLength = 1;
        int bestLength = 1;

        for (int i = 1; i < static_cast<int>(nums.size()); ++i) {
            if (nums[i] == nums[i - 1]) {
                continue;
            }

            if (nums[i] == nums[i - 1] + 1) {
                ++currentLength;
            } else {
                currentLength = 1;
            }

            bestLength = max(bestLength, currentLength);
        }

        return bestLength;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: O(n log n) because of sorting.
Space complexity: O(1) extra space, apart from the sort's internal stack.

The array can also be viewed as separate groups. Each unique number starts in its own group. When `number - 1` or `number + 1` has already appeared, the matching groups are joined. The largest group size is the answer.

Dry run: with `[5, 4, 7, 6]`, adding 5 creates one group, 4 joins it, 7 creates another group, and 6 joins both groups. The final group size is 4.

```java
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

class Solution {
    public int longestConsecutive(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        int[] parent = new int[n];
        int[] size = new int[n];
        Map<Integer, Integer> indexByValue = new HashMap<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
            parent[i] = i;
            size[i] = 1;
        }

        int answer = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
            int value = nums[i];
            if (indexByValue.containsKey(value)) {
                continue;
            }

            indexByValue.put(value, i);
            answer = Math.max(answer, 1);

            if (indexByValue.containsKey(value - 1)) {
                answer = Math.max(answer, union(i, indexByValue.get(value - 1), parent, size));
            }

            if (indexByValue.containsKey(value + 1)) {
                answer = Math.max(answer, union(i, indexByValue.get(value + 1), parent, size));
            }
        }

        return answer;
    }

    private int find(int node, int[] parent) {
        if (parent[node] != node) {
            parent[node] = find(parent[node], parent);
        }
        return parent[node];
    }

    private int union(int first, int second, int[] parent, int[] size) {
        int rootFirst = find(first, parent);
        int rootSecond = find(second, parent);

        if (rootFirst == rootSecond) {
            return size[rootFirst];
        }

        if (size[rootFirst] < size[rootSecond]) {
            int temporary = rootFirst;
            rootFirst = rootSecond;
            rootSecond = temporary;
        }

        parent[rootSecond] = rootFirst;
        size[rootFirst] += size[rootSecond];
        return size[rootFirst];
    }
}
```

The map prevents duplicate values from becoming separate nodes. An empty array naturally leaves `answer` as 0.

Time complexity: O(n alpha(n)) average, where alpha grows very slowly.
Space complexity: O(n) for the map and the union-find arrays.

## Intuition
If the array is sorted, all consecutive numbers will appear next to each other.
So the problem reduces to finding the longest contiguous increasing sequence where each element differs by exactly 1.

However, two things must be handled carefully:

Duplicates should be ignored (they neither break nor extend a sequence).

When the sequence breaks, we must reset the current streak and track the maximum seen so far.

## Approach
If the array is empty, return 0.

Sort the array.

Initialize:

currentLength = 1 to track the ongoing consecutive sequence

maxLength = 1 to store the longest sequence found

Iterate from index 1 to n-1:

If the current element is the same as the previous, skip it.

If the current element is exactly 1 greater than the previous, extend the sequence.

Otherwise, reset the current sequence length to 1.

Update maxLength at each step.

Return maxLength.

## Solution
```java[]
class Solution {
    public int longestConsecutive(int[] nums) {
        if(nums.length == 0){
            return 0;
        }

        Arrays.sort(nums);

        int maxLength = 1;
        int currentLength =1;

        for(int i=1;i<nums.length;i++){
            if(nums[i] == nums[i-1]){
                continue;
            }

            if(nums[i] ==nums[i-1]+1){
                currentLength++;
            }
            else{
                currentLength = 1;
            }

            maxLength=Math.max(currentLength,maxLength);
        }
        return maxLength;
    }
}
