# Approach (Sorting)

### Intuition
- Sort the array so that consecutive numbers become adjacent.
- Traverse the sorted array while keeping track of the current consecutive sequence.
- Ignore duplicates since they do not contribute to the sequence length.
- If the current element is exactly `1` greater than the previous unique element, extend the sequence.
- Otherwise, start a new sequence.
- Keep updating the maximum sequence length.

---

# Algorithm

1. Sort the array.
2. Initialize:
   - `longest = 1` to store the maximum sequence length.
   - `current_counter = 0` to count the current sequence.
   - `last_Smaller = INT_MIN` to store the previous unique element.
3. Traverse the sorted array:
   - If `nums[i] - 1 == last_Smaller`, increment `current_counter`.
   - Else if `nums[i] != last_Smaller` (ignore duplicates):
     - Reset `current_counter = 1`.
     - Update `last_Smaller = nums[i]`.
   - Update `longest`.
4. Return `longest`.

---

## Solution
```cpp[]
class Solution {
public:
    int longestConsecutive(vector<int>& nums) {
        int n = nums.size();
        // sort the array
        sort(nums.begin(), nums.end());

        int longest = 1;             // longest sequence
        int current_counter = 0;     // current sequence length
        int last_Smaller = INT_MIN;  // track last smaller element

        for(int i = 0; i<n; i++){
            if(nums[i] - 1 == last_Smaller){ // if consecutive number exists
                current_counter++;           // increase counter 
                last_Smaller = nums[i];      // update last smaller
            }
            else if(nums[i] != last_Smaller){ // if no consecutive number exists
                // reset the current_counter to 1
                current_counter = 1;
                // udpate last smaller to it 
                last_Smaller = nums[i];

            }
            longest = max(longest, current_counter);
        }
        return longest;
    }
};
```
---

# Complexity Analysis

- **Time Complexity:** `O(n log n)`
  - Sorting takes `O(n log n)`.
  - Single traversal takes `O(n)`.

- **Space Complexity:** `O(1)`
  - No extra data structures are used (excluding the sorting implementation).

